<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christchurch Call</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2019">2019</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> Call is an international initiative launched in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in May 2019 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacinda-ardern">Jacinda Ardern</a> and French President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-macron">Emmanuel Macron</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch-mosque-shootings">Christchurch mosque shootings</a>, which had been livestreamed online. Governments and technology companies that support it commit to work to eliminate terrorist and violent extremist content on the internet while protecting a free and open internet.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The initiative began as the Christchurch Call to Action Summit, held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 15 May 2019, two months after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch-mosque-shootings">Christchurch mosque shootings</a> of 15 March 2019. It was proposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacinda-ardern">Jacinda Ardern</a>, then Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, and co-chaired by her and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-macron">Emmanuel Macron</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its stated aim was to bring countries and technology companies together to end the use of social media to organise and promote terrorism and violent extremism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The pledge is a non-binding agreement in three parts: commitments for governments, commitments for online service providers, and ways in which the two can work together. Seventeen countries signed it at first and another 31 followed on 24 September 2019. Signatories include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>. Amazon, Dailymotion, Facebook, Google, Microsoft, Qwant, Twitter and YouTube signed as members of the Global Internet Forum to Counter Terrorism.</p><p>The government of President Trump declined to attend in 2019, citing concerns that compliance could conflict with free-speech protections in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>, though it supported the summit's overarching message. On 7 May 2021 the White House press secretary, Jen Psaki, announced that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> under President Biden would join, taking part in a virtual summit on 14 May 2021.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 4 April 2023 Prime Minister Chris Hipkins appointed Ardern Special Envoy for the Christchurch Call, an unpaid role reporting to him. On 14 May 2024 Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-luxon">Christopher Luxon</a> and Macron agreed that the Call would continue as a charity rather than as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet, and announced a new non-governmental body, the Christchurch Call Foundation, to coordinate its work.</p><p>Reactions to the summit were divided. InternetNZ's chief executive Jordan Carter called it a vital first step, and Jillian York of the Electronic Frontier Foundation welcomed its call for transparency about moderation while questioning how governments define terrorism and violent extremism. Tom Rogan in the Washington Examiner argued that it would breach Americans' First Amendment rights, and Nick Gillespie of Reason objected to governments and corporations deciding together what speech was acceptable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christchurch Call” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
